    "dc.legislation.history" : [ {
      "value" : "Legislative History: \nThe Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023  \n\nSource: Official Record of Parliament, India Code, and Ministry of Home Affairs  \n\nAct Title - The Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 \nAct Number - Act No. 46 of 2023 \nAct ID (India Code) - 202346 \n Presidential Assent date\tDecember 25, 2023 \n Enforcement Date\tJuly 01, 2024 \nNodal Ministry\tMinistry of Home Affairs \n\nPrecedent & Historical Rationale\n\n●\tRepeal of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973: Repeals the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973 (2 of 1974) to establish a modern, technology-driven procedural framework focused on speedy trial timelines and citizen-centric rights.\n●\tMandatory Timelines & Tech Integration: Mandates electronic submission of evidence and summonses, introduces Zero FIR and mandatory forensic visits for serious offences (punishable by 7+ years), and imposes strict statutory deadlines for investigation, charge sheet filing, and judgment delivery.\n \nParliamentary Trajectory & Enactment\n●\tFormulation: Introduced by the Union Minister of Home Affairs, Shri Amit Shah, as part of the core legislative package to replace colonial-era criminal procedural laws.\n●\tParliamentary Passage: Introduced in the Lok Sabha on August 11, 2023; referred to the Parliamentary Standing Committee on Home Affairs; re-introduced as Bill No. 174 of 2023 on December 12, 2023; passed by the Lok Sabha on December 20, 2023; and passed by the Rajya Sabha on December 21, 2023.\n●\tPresidential Assent: Received the assent of the President of India on December 25, 2023.\n●\tEnforcement: Enforced on July 01, 2024.",
